          "path": "../manifest.json",
          "url": "https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202243791"
        },
        "falseColor": false,
        "notes": "Convex light-curve reconstruction with approximate thermal size: 95.2 km effective diameter (catalog ±1.36 km; additional shape and thermal-model uncertainty). Thermal size is used as a volume-scale approximation. Both pole solutions remain possible. No surface imagery exists; rotational phase is illustrative. The physical size is approximately calibrated by assigning the AKARI thermal effective diameter to the mesh volume-equivalent diameter. Neither source mesh nor spin is modified. Convex inversion supplies only broad outline; no resolved craters, concavities, reflectance or regolith texture can be inferred. Published convex inversion from Hanuš et al. (2013). The selected archive solution is retained as a reproducible coarse model. An alternative pole remains possible; no unique orientation is claimed. The release supplies no registered global surface imagery or measured local terrain. AKARI thermal effective diameter provides only an explicit volume-scale approximation. One mean color for the whole disc (Gaia DR3 reflectance spectrum), scaled to the 4.4% geometric albedo JPL lists; no terrain, albedo pattern or color variation is drawn."
      },
